{"id":"babylon","name":"Babylon","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:babylon","torrey:babylon"],"groups":["Called also CHALDEA, which see"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Babylon","text":"The Greek form of BABEL; Semitic form Babilu, meaning “The Gate of God.” In the Assyrian tablets it means “The city of the dispersion of the tribes.” The monumental list of its kings reaches back to B.C. 2300, and includes Khammurabi, or Amraphel (q.v.), the contemporary of Abraham. It stood on the Euphrates, about 200 miles above its junction with the Tigris, which flowed through its midst and divided it into two almost equal parts. The Elamites invaded Chaldea (i.e., Lower Mesopotamia, or Shinar, and Upper Mesopotamia, or Accad, now combined into one) and held it in subjection. At length Khammu-rabi delivered it from the foreign yoke, and founded the new empire of Chaldea (q.v.), making Babylon the capital of the united kingdom. This city gradually grew in extent and grandeur, but in process of time it became subject to Assyria. On the fall of Nineveh (B.C. 606) it threw off the …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H894","lemma":"בָּבֶל","xlit":"Bâbel","gloss":"Babel , including Babylonia and the Babylonian empire","kjv":"Babel, Babylon","count":102,"share":0.21},{"strong":"H3778","lemma":"כַּשְׂדִּי","xlit":"Kasdîy","gloss":"a Kasdite, or descendant of Kesed","kjv":"Chaldeans, Chaldees, inhabitants of Chaldea","count":28,"share":0.06},{"strong":"H4430","lemma":"מֶלֶךְ","xlit":"melek","gloss":"a king","kjv":"king, royal","count":30,"share":0.06},{"strong":"H5019","lemma":"נְבוּכַדְנֶאצַּר","xlit":"Nᵉbûwkadneʼtstsar","gloss":"Nebukadnetstsar , king of Babylon","kjv":"Nebuchadnezzar, Nebuchadrezzar","count":26,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H5002","lemma":"נְאֻם","xlit":"nᵉʼum","gloss":"an oracle","kjv":"said, saith","count":26,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H5922","lemma":"עַל","xlit":"ʻal","gloss":"{above, over, upon, or against in a great variety of app","kjv":"ab